module bindings.javascript.string_replacement_template_argument;

// When the replacement argument of String.prototype.replace or
// String.prototype.replaceAll is a string, the platform treats it as a
// substitution template rather than literal text: `$&` names the match,
// `` $` `` the subject before it, `$'` the subject after it, `$$` a literal
// dollar, and `$n` the regex capture groups. Data that reaches that
// argument therefore decides more than the characters it contributes --
// `$'` splices everything after the match into the substitution position,
// so a value spliced into serialized payload text can pull the rest of the
// payload across the quoting that value was given. The documented literal
// form is the function replacement, whose return value is substituted
// verbatim with no template interpretation.
//
// The fact recorded here is the argument's interpretation, so the sink sits
// on the argument a caller's data flows into. A function passed in that
// position is control flow rather than data -- nothing flows into the
// argument node itself -- so the literal form needs no separate clause. The
// key is the method name alone, matching the character-filter binding that
// already labels this call, and the pattern side of the call is left out of
// the predicate because the template interpretation is documented for string
// and regex patterns alike.
binding stringReplacementTemplateArgument {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.method in ["replace", "replaceAll"]
  emit sink code.FormatString at args[1]
}
